One events resource object: a document added to the account's license. This is the deliverability
event, the only kind of event in this fixture set for which ABEvent.IsDeliveryEvent is true.

Structure and semantics:
  https://developerdocs.instructure.com/services/ab-connect/reference/events.md
  which names "added" and "removed" as change_type values and "document" as a target, and states:
  "Unlike Standard change Events, deliverability Events should be acted upon immediately to ensure
  your system is in line with license agreements".
  01-sdk-v3.md section 7.3, which turns that sentence into IsDeliveryEvent.
Gate G-4 confirmed both "added" and "document" occur live on devel partner sws, 2026-08-02.

Everything else here is assembled, not observed live: the event id, seq, and date_utc. The document
GUID is the one gate G-3 used, reused so tests can join this event to the standards fixtures.

Deliberate omissions, each of which a test should assert on:
  - There is no "relationships" member, so ABEvent.Relationships must be null. A delivery event names
    no standard.
  - There is no "affected_properties" member, so ABEventAttributes.AffectedProperties must be NULL,
    not empty. Compare event-deleted-standard.json, where it is present and empty and must be an
    empty list. Section 7.1's convention is that null means the array was absent and empty means it
    was present and empty; these two fixtures together are what stop an implementation from
    collapsing them.
  - There is no "section_guid" member, which is correct for a document-target event.
